// TEST: Idx Card Not Static (rejects)
// ============================================================================
// INDEX-ELIGIBILITY IS OPT-IN. A plain `struct` is never index-eligible, no
// matter how eligible its SHAPE is: `CGm112` below has bounded Int fields and
// a foldable conjunct — it is structurally perfect — and `idx_card` still
// refuses it, because it was not declared `static struct`.
//
//   struct CGm112 is not declared static — write `static struct CGm112` to
//   make it index-eligible
//
// This is a deliberate design ruling, not an implementation limitation, so it
// gets a test rather than a comment. Two reasons decided it:
//
//   1. THE EMPTINESS WARNING. A derived-empty solution set warns, because a
//      user who believed their constraints satisfiable has written a no-op
//      `range<R>`. That warning is only meaningful for a struct the user
//      INTENDED as an index. Under structural eligibility it would fire at
//      ordinary constrained records that never asked to be index types —
//      turning a real signal into noise.
//   2. NO ACTION AT A DISTANCE. Under structural eligibility, adding a bound
//      to the last field of an unrelated struct would silently PROMOTE it to
//      an index type and removing one would silently DEMOTE it. The plan
//      commits to nominal, deliberate identity for these types (§3, "NOMINAL
//      identity by struct name"); deriving eligibility implicitly would make
//      the most nominal thing in the design the least explicit.
//
// This does not break the plan's §1 "no new former, no new keyword" promise.
// The promise is that there is no new TYPE FORMER — you never write
// `ConstrainedIdx<CGm112>`; the struct name IS the index type name, and that
// is intact. And `static` is not a new keyword in Blade: it already spells the
// compile-time world in `let static`, `static function` and `static
// method_for`. `static struct` uses it to mean exactly what it means
// everywhere else.
//
// The accepting twin is index-types/145, which is this file with one keyword
// added.
// ============================================================================
struct CGm112 {
    m1:    Int<min=-1, max=1>,
    m2:    Int<min=-1, max=1>,
    m_out: Int<min=-1, max=1>
} where m1 + m2 == m_out

let static CARD = idx_card(CGm112)
